  :coscientist/subject "Open item from iteration 61: the nvgpu (h2, f16-activation) dense result was argmax-exact only. parity_check.py made oracle-only (llama-server json optional, exit 1 on a top-1 mismatch, dense rows sliced from the 2048-wide buffer). Qwen2.5-0.5B Q8_0 24 L fn guests on Xavier, 14 steps: h2 layout x rel 2.3e-3..6.9e-3, last-step logits max|diff| 9.4e-3, KL(oracle||gpu) 1.06e-7 nats, 14/14 argmax; exact s4 layout x rel <= 2.4e-5, KL 9.2e-13, 14/14. The h2 KL is 4 orders below llama.cpp's own deviation from the f64 oracle on this file (1.5e-3) -- not a quality problem -- but s4 is also FASTER on the dense model (fn 14.6 vs 15.9 ms/step) and, once the exact table also reads the lm_head once (:lm-once added to nvgpu-exact), faster or equal at every batch size: adaptive B=1/2/4 14.83/19.15/29.60 vs h2 15.72/19.17/29.84 (s4 without lm-once: 35.61 at B=4). Generator rule: dense on nvgpu -> the exact table; Nex nvgpu guest byte-identical, dense guest byte-identical to the measured nvgpu-exact guest"
  :coscientist/engine-note "The layout table's second reversal on a dense model, and this time the tuned kernel lost on both axes: the f16 activation path pays for itself only where the tensor is wide enough to be bandwidth-bound (Nex, 2048 x 8192); a 896-wide layer is launch-bound and the conversions are overhead. The check that was owed since iteration 61 (distribution, not argmax) is what made the comparison decidable -- and the result is that the approximate path is both fine and unnecessary here."
